<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alexander Romance</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Alexander Romance is a legendary account of the life of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>, falsely attributed to his historian Callisthenes and compiled in Greek by the 3rd century CE from earlier material. It makes Alexander the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nectanebo-ii">Nectanebo II</a>, the last native <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. Translated and adapted into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac">Syriac</a>, Persian, Ethiopic and many other languages, it became one of the most widely read stories of the medieval world.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alexander-romance.jpg" alt="Alexander Romance" width="320" height="496" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alexander_romance._Armenian_illuminated_manuscript_of_XIV_century_(Venice,_San_Lazzaro,_424).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Alexander Romance has been called antiquity's most successful novel. Its author is uncertain, though some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> attribute it pseudonymously to Alexander's court historian Callisthenes, so the writer is commonly called Pseudo-Callisthenes. The original was composed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koine-greek">Koine Greek</a>, and the oldest Greek version, the alpha recension, can be dated to the 3rd century CE and survives in a single manuscript. It was the source of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> version by Julius Valerius Alexander Polemius in the 4th century and of an Armenian version in the 5th. Because so many works derive from it, the Alexander romance is sometimes treated as a genre rather than a single text, and none of its recensions can be considered canonical.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The book follows Alexander from his birth to his accession in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a>, his conquest of the Persian Empire and his death. Although built around a historical core, it is mostly fantastical. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nectanebo-ii">Nectanebo II</a>, the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, flees to the Macedonian court as a magician, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olympia">Olympias</a>, wife of Philip II, becomes pregnant by him. Alexander kills Nectanebo as a boy and is taught by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a>.</p><p>Later episodes drew wide notice in their own right. In the Gates of Alexander, Alexander prays and two mountains draw together; he builds gates of bronze that enclose twenty-two nations, among them Gog and Magog. In the story of the Fountain of Life, shaped by earlier legends of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilgamesh">Gilgamesh</a>, his cook Andreas washes a fish in a fountain, the fish springs to life, and Andreas drinks the water. Alexander punishes him and the daughter who shared it, turning them into spirits of the sea and the desert. The Land of Darkness grew from a few statements in the original, especially in versions from Islamic lands.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>In premodern times the Romance underwent more than 100 translations, elaborations and derivations in dozens of languages, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a> to Malaysia, among them Coptic, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geez">Ge'ez</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac">Syriac</a>, Arabic, Persian, Armenian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a>. Alexander was assimilated into the cultures of his readers: a Christian in Christian legends, a Muslim in Islamic ones. The story of Dhu al-Qarnayn in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> is generally seen as an allusive summary of the tradition as the Syriac Alexander Legend gave it.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, Leo the Archpriest found a Greek copy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> and made a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> version, on which the Historia de Proeliis was based. It went through three recensions between the twelfth and fifteenth centuries and was translated more often over the next three centuries than any text except the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospels">Gospels</a>. Vernacular versions followed in French, Middle English, Irish and Old Church Slavonic. In Persian the story reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdowsi">Ferdowsi</a>'s Shahnameh and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizami-ganjavi">Nizami</a>'s Iskandarnameh. The first modern English translation was by E. H. Haight in 1955, and Richard Stoneman's of 1991 is the major one.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Alexander Romance” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Armenian literary tradition begins in the 5th century, with a translation of the Bible, its oldest surviving text, and the Armenian <b>Alexander Romance</b>, translated in the same century.</blockquote><a href="/language/armenian">Armenian · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>…Humbaba alongside the Watchers and giants, and the quest of Alexander the Great for the Fountain of Life in the <b>Alexander Romance</b> was inspired by Gilgamesh's search for eternal youth, though there the water of life replaces the plant and a fish returning to life replaces the serpent.</blockquote><a href="/work/epic-of-gilgamesh">Epic of Gilgamesh · Reception</a></li><li><blockquote>It introduced the <b>Alexander Romance</b> into Persian, turning Alexander from the negative figure of Zoroastrian writings into a positive one; the Iskandarnameh of Nizami and the Ayina-i Iskandari of Amir Khusrau owe much to it.</blockquote><a href="/work/shahnameh">Shahnameh · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Nectanebo II was the last native pharaoh, later turned into Alexander the Great's father in the <b>Alexander Romance</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/thirtieth-dynasty-of-egypt">Thirtieth Dynasty of Egypt</a></li></ul></section>
